          'Now absorb: $ab + abc = ab(1 + c) = ab \\cdot 1 = ab$, and $a^{\\prime}c + a^{\\prime}bc = a^{\\prime}c(1 + b) = a^{\\prime}c$.',
          'Hence the left side equals $ab + a^{\\prime}c$; the reverse inequality is immediate since the left side contains the two surviving terms. $\\blacksquare$',
          'Reading: if $b$ and $c$ are both 1, then either $a$ is 1 (so $ab$ holds) or $a^{\\prime}$ is 1 (so $a^{\\prime}c$ holds). The $bc$ term is never the only reason the function is 1, so it can go.',
